[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Anti-Patterns: God Object, Spaghetti Code, and The Golden Hammer"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In software engineering, knowing what not to do is just as important as knowing design patterns. Anti-patterns are common responses to recurring problems that usually yield negative consequences. This tutorial explores three of the most notorious anti-patterns: the God Object, Spaghetti Code, and The Golden Hammer."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. The God Object"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"A God Object (or God Class) refers to a single object that knows too much or does too much. It violates the Single Responsibility Principle (SRP) by centralizing most of the system's business logic, state, and behavior into one monolithic class."},{"order":5,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High coupling and low cohesion across the entire application."},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Extreme difficulty in writing unit tests due to vast dependencies."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"High risk of merge conflicts when multiple developers modify the same file."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"class ApplicationManager {
  constructor() {
    this.databaseConnection = {};
    this.userSession = {};
    this.logger = {};
    this.billingService = {};
  }
  handleEverything() {
    // Connects to DB, processes payments, logs errors, and renders UI
  }
}"},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Spaghetti Code"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Spaghetti Code is characterized by complex, tangled, and unstructured control paths. It often features excessive use of nested conditional statements, global variables, and unstructured jumps, making the execution flow resemble a plate of spaghetti."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Unclear execution flow making debugging a tedious process."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Absence of modularity or encapsulation."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"function processOrder(order) {
  if (order != null) {
    if (order.status === 'pending') {
      if (order.user.isVerified) {
        if (inventory.checkStock(order.item)) {
          // Deeply nested logic
          console.log('Processing...');
        } else {
          return 'Out of stock';
        }
      } else {
        return 'User not verified';
      }
    }
  }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"3. The Golden Hammer"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Golden Hammer anti-pattern occurs when a developer or team overuses a familiar technology or design pattern to solve every single problem, regardless of whether it is the right tool for the job. Popularized by Abraham Maslow's phrase: If all you have is a hammer, everything looks like a nail."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Forcing a relational database to handle real-time streaming data."},{"order":17,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Using a complex design pattern (like Abstract Factory) for a trivial object instantiation."},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion and Refactoring Strategies"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Recognizing these anti-patterns is the first step toward clean architecture. To combat the God Object, apply domain-driven design and break classes down by responsibility. Eliminate spaghetti code by applying guard clauses, extracting functions, and adhering to standard control flows. Finally, avoid the Golden Hammer by continuously expanding your technical toolkit and evaluating each problem objectively."}]
